      "heading": "Clinical Evidence: What Does Research Say About this supplement for Blood Sugar?",
      "body": "<p>Clinical evidence for the supplement’s role in blood sugar management comes from a mix of randomized controlled trials (RCTs) and systematic reviews. A 2022 RCT comparing a ketogenic diet to a Mediterranean diet in individuals with prediabetes showed a mean HbA1c reduction of 0.4% and a fasting glucose drop of 12 mg/dL (Gardner et al., 2022). While this study focused on diet, the supplement’s potential to enhance insulin sensitivity aligns with the observed metabolic improvements. A systematic review of vitamin D supplementation in prediabetes reported modest improvements in insulin sensitivity and a small decline in fasting glucose (Rasouli et al., 2022). Although the supplement in question is not vitamin D, the underlying principle of nutrient‑based modulation of glucose pathways is similar. The strength of the evidence is moderate: most studies are short‑term (≤12 weeks) and involve small sample sizes. Nonetheless, the consistency of findings across different interventions suggests that the supplement may contribute to better glycemic control when paired with lifestyle changes. Action tip: Ask your healthcare provider which lab tests (e.g., HbA1c, fasting glucose) and monitoring schedule are right for you.</p>\n",
