      "heading": "Side Effects, Precautions, and Warnings for Blood Sugar",
      "body": "<p>Common side effects reported in early studies include mild gastrointestinal upset, bloating, and occasional diarrhea. These symptoms are typically transient and resolve as the body adapts to the new supplement. Patients with elevated blood sugar should monitor for these symptoms during the first two weeks and adjust the dose if needed. The underlying mechanism is thought to involve osmotic effects of the supplement’s active components on the intestinal lumen, which can draw water into the gut and stimulate motility. In most cases, the benefit of improved glycemic control outweighs these temporary inconveniences, but clinicians should counsel patients to take the supplement with food or a small snack to mitigate discomfort.</p>\n<p><strong>Drug Interaction Table</strong> <strong>Interaction with Common Medications</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table class=\"md-table\"><thead><tr><th class=\"tbl-th\">Medication</th><th class=\"tbl-th\">Interaction Type</th><th class=\"tbl-th\">Recommendation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class=\"tbl-row-even\"><td class=\"tbl-td\">ACE inhibitors</td><td class=\"tbl-td\">May increase blood pressure variability</td><td class=\"tbl-td\">Consult your prescribing doctor before combining</td></tr><tr class=\"tbl-row-odd\"><td class=\"tbl-td\">ARBs</td><td class=\"tbl-td\">Potential additive effects on kidney function</td><td class=\"tbl-td\">Consult your prescribing doctor before combining</td></tr><tr class=\"tbl-row-even\"><td class=\"tbl-td\">Insulin</td><td class=\"tbl-td\">May increase hypoglycaemia risk</td><td class=\"tbl-td\">Consult your prescribing doctor before combining</td></tr><tr class=\"tbl-row-odd\"><td class=\"tbl-td\">Statins</td><td class=\"tbl-td\">Possible mild liver enzyme elevation</td><td class=\"tbl-td\">Consult your prescribing doctor before combining</td></tr><tr class=\"tbl-row-even\"><td class=\"tbl-td\">Metformin</td><td class=\"tbl-td\">No known interaction</td><td class=\"tbl-td\">Consult your prescribing doctor before combining</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"tbl-source\">Source: Kaur et al., 2020</p>\n<p><strong>Warning</strong>: If you experience persistent nausea, vomiting, or severe abdominal pain, stop the supplement and seek medical advice. These symptoms may indicate an intolerable gastrointestinal reaction or, in rare cases, an allergic response. Always discuss any new supplement with your healthcare provider, especially if you have kidney or liver conditions. Action tip: Review all current medications with your doctor before starting. This step is crucial because the supplement’s active compounds can alter the pharmacokinetics of concurrently used drugs, potentially leading to subtherapeutic effects or toxicity. For example, the supplement’s mild diuretic effect may enhance the blood pressure-lowering action of ACE inhibitors or ARBs, necessitating closer monitoring of blood pressure and renal function. Similarly, when combined with insulin, the additive glucose-lowering effect can precipitate hypoglycaemia, particularly in patients who already titrate insulin doses. By proactively addressing these interactions, patients can safely incorporate the supplement into a comprehensive prediabetes workplace health plan that balances metabolic benefits with minimal adverse events.</p>\n",
