      "heading": "What the Critics Say — Limitations of Research on this supplement for Blood Sugar",
      "body": "<p>Critics point out that most studies on the supplement are short‑term (≤12 weeks) and involve small sample sizes, limiting the ability to assess long‑term safety and efficacy. Some trials report no significant difference in HbA1c compared to placebo, suggesting that the supplement’s effect may be modest or dependent on adherence to diet and exercise. Non‑responder rates are also a concern; individuals with higher baseline insulin resistance may not experience the same benefits. Additionally, the heterogeneity of study designs—varying doses, formulations, and participant characteristics—makes it difficult to draw firm conclusions. > Bottom Line for Skeptics: While early data are promising, the supplement’s role in managing blood sugar remains adjunctive. It should not replace proven therapies such as metformin or lifestyle interventions without professional guidance. Action tip: Consult your healthcare provider before making changes to your routine.</p>\n",
